{"kind":"group","id":"98f9eb5b-af28-4320-b669-52e4a52234e1","type":"c552a431-af5b-46a9-a8a4-0fcbc27ef596","name":"Group","nick":"8 - Buckle","desc":"A group of Grasshopper objects","box":[0,0,0,0],"colour":[40,42,45,49],"members":["1140f52d-bf6c-46e7-9552-29c4b9957777","4fb856b5-508d-4d92-adef-002e556d1485","8d638f07-6180-45fc-9ace-bdbffbb5d9ea","cc8f9e7b-857c-4d90-8e92-c241705566dd"]},
{"kind":"panel","id":"84d638cb-9b0c-4511-a7d8-0a11415126b8","type":"59e0b89a-e487-49f8-bab8-b5bab16be14c","name":"Panel","nick":"A printed cylinder","desc":"A panel for custom notes and text values","box":[0,-360,560,300],"src":[],"text":"A 3D printed cylinder, checked for buckling while it is printed.\n\nTU/e's cylinder (Wolfs, Bos and Salet 2018): radius 250 mm, layers 40 x 10 mm, 5 m/min, a mortar whose stiffness grows with age, E = 0.078 + 0.0012 t MPa. Move Time: more layers are printed, the bottom ones older and stiffer, and the load factor on the concrete's own weight falls. It reaches 1 - the wall buckles - at about 47 layers.\n\nTheir lab cylinders fell at 25 to 31 layers, by buckling and by the concrete yielding at the base; this is the elastic buckling.\n\nPrinted Layers, Fresh Concrete and Surface Connection are GhPython components: open them to see, and change, how the bricks are cut, how the concrete stiffens and what a joint is.\n\nTry: Joint 1 mm and a lower Ks lets the layers slip; Round 0 squares the filament; Speed lower gives each layer time to stiffen.","colour":[0,0,0,0]}
],
